WEBVTT

0
00:00.239 --> 00:01.572
<v Krista>In this video, we're talking about</v>

1
00:01.572 --> 00:02.688
decimal arithmetic,

2
00:02.688 --> 00:05.865
or addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division

3
00:05.865 --> 00:06.698
of decimals.

4
00:06.698 --> 00:08.375
So, let's look at addition here,

5
00:08.375 --> 00:09.208
of decimals.

6
00:09.208 --> 00:11.902
This is going to be just like the addition that we're used to.

7
00:11.902 --> 00:13.210
The only thing we have to worry about when

8
00:13.210 --> 00:14.393
we're dealing with decimals,

9
00:14.393 --> 00:17.058
is we need to make sure we line up the decimal place.

10
00:17.058 --> 00:18.491
So here we have 4.5,

11
00:18.491 --> 00:19.590
so we'll go ahead and put

12
00:19.590 --> 00:20.573
4.5.

13
00:20.573 --> 00:22.103
And then we have 3.34,

14
00:22.103 --> 00:24.891
so we need to make sure that our decimal places line up.

15
00:24.891 --> 00:26.723
So we wouldn't line up the four here,

16
00:26.723 --> 00:27.778
with this five,

17
00:27.778 --> 00:29.688
because then our decimals wouldn't line up.

18
00:29.688 --> 00:32.017
We need to make sure that the decimals

19
00:32.017 --> 00:34.502
line up vertically like this with each other.

20
00:34.502 --> 00:36.215
If we have any values that are hanging off

21
00:36.215 --> 00:37.539
the right hand side here,

22
00:37.539 --> 00:39.318
we can always just put in this

23
00:39.318 --> 00:40.695
zero if we need to, right?

24
00:40.695 --> 00:42.262
4.5 is the same as

25
00:42.262 --> 00:43.400
4.50.

26
00:43.400 --> 00:44.736
If it helps you to write the zero

27
00:44.736 --> 00:46.072
to remember that it's there,

28
00:46.072 --> 00:46.905
then you can do that.

29
00:46.905 --> 00:48.152
Otherwise, we just need to know that

30
00:48.152 --> 00:49.967
four plus zero is still four.

31
00:49.967 --> 00:51.408
So we add from the right-hand side

32
00:51.408 --> 00:52.378
like we normally would.

33
00:52.378 --> 00:54.049
Four plus zero gives us four.

34
00:54.049 --> 00:55.661
Five plus three gives us eight.

35
00:55.661 --> 00:57.369
We carry down our decimal place

36
00:57.369 --> 00:59.466
exactly in line with where it is here,

37
00:59.466 --> 01:01.782
and then four plus three gives us seven.

38
01:01.782 --> 01:04.032
So the result is 7.84.

39
01:04.032 --> 01:06.005
We do the same thing when we have subtraction.

40
01:06.005 --> 01:08.388
So, here we have 16.7

41
01:08.388 --> 01:09.221
minus

42
01:09.221 --> 01:10.229
2.26.

43
01:10.229 --> 01:12.545
So, we want to make sure we line up our decimal places.

44
01:12.545 --> 01:15.620
So we're going to put 2.26 in here,

45
01:15.620 --> 01:18.331
make sure our decimal places line up like this,

46
01:18.331 --> 01:19.768
and now, because we're doing subtraction,

47
01:19.768 --> 01:22.733
we might really want to put this zero in here,

48
01:22.733 --> 01:24.579
because we're going to say zero minus six,

49
01:24.579 --> 01:25.412
we can't do that,

50
01:25.412 --> 01:27.276
so we have to borrow one from the seven.

51
01:27.276 --> 01:28.736
So the seven is going to become six.

52
01:28.736 --> 01:30.073
We're going to put the one here.

53
01:30.073 --> 01:32.616
Ten minus six is going to give us four.

54
01:32.616 --> 01:34.712
Six minus two is four, here.

55
01:34.712 --> 01:36.208
Six minus two is

56
01:36.208 --> 01:37.124
four,

57
01:37.124 --> 01:39.127
and one minus zero, or just one,

58
01:39.127 --> 01:40.001
we get one here.

59
01:40.001 --> 01:42.270
And notice how we brought the decimal place down

60
01:42.270 --> 01:45.805
directly in line with where we had it in our 16.7

61
01:45.805 --> 01:47.030
and our 2.26,

62
01:47.030 --> 01:48.947
so the result is 14.44.

63
01:49.832 --> 01:51.919
Now when it comes to decimal multiplication,

64
01:51.919 --> 01:53.877
this is going to be slightly different.

65
01:53.877 --> 01:54.710
We're going to,

66
01:54.710 --> 01:56.076
when we multiply,

67
01:56.076 --> 01:58.653
ignore the decimals for the first part of our problem.

68
01:58.653 --> 02:00.552
So, let's take the number with more digits.

69
02:00.552 --> 02:02.971
This one, 6.21, has three digits.

70
02:02.971 --> 02:04.834
This 2.3 only has two digits.

71
02:04.834 --> 02:07.500
We'll take the one with the larger number of digits

72
02:07.500 --> 02:08.653
and put that at the top,

73
02:08.653 --> 02:09.486
so 6.21.

74
02:10.588 --> 02:12.072
And then we have our 2.3,

75
02:12.072 --> 02:12.961
so we'll put that here,

76
02:12.961 --> 02:13.831
2.3.

77
02:13.831 --> 02:16.360
So, in this case you notice that we right-align everything.

78
02:16.360 --> 02:18.135
We don't line up our decimal places,

79
02:18.135 --> 02:19.559
we just right-align everything.

80
02:19.559 --> 02:20.522
And now we're going to do this

81
02:20.522 --> 02:22.689
like a regular multiplication problem,

82
02:22.689 --> 02:24.531
and we're going to pretend that the decimal places

83
02:24.531 --> 02:25.686
aren't even there.

84
02:25.686 --> 02:27.049
So, we're going to take this value here,

85
02:27.049 --> 02:27.882
three,

86
02:27.882 --> 02:30.572
and multiply by each of the numbers in this 6.21 here.

87
02:30.572 --> 02:32.561
So, we're going to say three times one gives us three.

88
02:32.561 --> 02:34.836
Three times two gives us six,

89
02:34.836 --> 02:37.209
and three times six gives us 18.

90
02:37.209 --> 02:38.859
Remember now, we put a zero here,

91
02:38.859 --> 02:40.727
and then we multiply two by one,

92
02:40.727 --> 02:42.310
then by two, then by six.

93
02:42.310 --> 02:44.075
So, two times one gives us two.

94
02:44.075 --> 02:45.711
Two times two gives us four,

95
02:45.711 --> 02:48.008
and two times six gives us twelve.

96
02:48.008 --> 02:49.808
Now we want to add these together.

97
02:49.808 --> 02:52.622
This is, again, just like a regular multiplication problem.

98
02:52.622 --> 02:54.118
Three plus zero is three.

99
02:54.118 --> 02:55.668
Six plus two is eight.

100
02:55.668 --> 02:57.088
Eight plus four is 12,

101
02:57.088 --> 02:57.979
carry the one.

102
02:57.979 --> 02:59.244
One plus one is two,

103
02:59.244 --> 03:00.608
plus two is four,

104
03:00.608 --> 03:02.676
and we have one out here in front.

105
03:02.676 --> 03:03.509
So now, we've done this

106
03:03.509 --> 03:05.233
like a regular multiplication problem.

107
03:05.233 --> 03:07.624
We completely ignored our decimals,

108
03:07.624 --> 03:08.716
but for our final answer,

109
03:08.716 --> 03:10.345
we need to put a decimal back in,

110
03:10.345 --> 03:11.560
and the way that we do that

111
03:11.560 --> 03:13.038
is we count the number of places

112
03:13.038 --> 03:15.094
that we have behind the decimal.

113
03:15.094 --> 03:16.482
So here we have one,

114
03:16.482 --> 03:18.515
two places behind the decimal.

115
03:18.515 --> 03:20.621
Here we have one place behind the decimal.

116
03:20.621 --> 03:23.434
That's three total places behind the decimal,

117
03:23.434 --> 03:25.347
so we count over in our final answer

118
03:25.347 --> 03:27.136
one, two, three,

119
03:27.136 --> 03:29.603
and we put the decimal point right here.

120
03:29.603 --> 03:31.444
So then our final answer becomes

121
03:31.444 --> 03:32.277
14.283.

122
03:34.773 --> 03:36.604
Now, for the last part of decimal arithmetic,

123
03:36.604 --> 03:38.517
we're going to do division with decimals.

124
03:38.517 --> 03:40.391
Here we have a decimal inside our dividend.

125
03:40.391 --> 03:42.113
We have 6.24.

126
03:42.113 --> 03:45.234
We want to just take this decimal and move it directly up

127
03:45.234 --> 03:46.683
into our quotient.

128
03:46.683 --> 03:49.061
So we're going to put the decimal right here,

129
03:49.061 --> 03:50.304
and then just ignore it, really,

130
03:50.304 --> 03:52.165
as we divide through by four.

131
03:52.165 --> 03:55.038
So, four goes into six one time.

132
03:55.038 --> 03:56.793
One times four is four.

133
03:56.793 --> 03:59.763
Six minus four is going to give us two.

134
03:59.763 --> 04:01.051
We bring the two

135
04:01.051 --> 04:01.884
down.

136
04:01.884 --> 04:04.035
Four goes into 22 five times,

137
04:04.035 --> 04:05.356
so we put the five there.

138
04:05.356 --> 04:07.577
Five times four is 20.

139
04:07.577 --> 04:08.410
When we subtract,

140
04:08.410 --> 04:09.655
we get two.

141
04:09.655 --> 04:12.956
And then we bring down our four to get 24.

142
04:12.956 --> 04:15.281
Four goes into 24 six times.

143
04:15.281 --> 04:17.492
Six times four is 24.

144
04:17.492 --> 04:19.392
When I subtract I get zero,

145
04:19.392 --> 04:20.625
so my remainder is zero,

146
04:20.625 --> 04:21.883
and my answer then is

147
04:21.883 --> 04:23.234
1.56

148
04:23.234 --> 04:24.767
because we just moved that decimal

149
04:24.767 --> 04:26.959
directly up from where it was here in the dividend,

150
04:26.959 --> 04:27.932
and it stays there,

151
04:27.932 --> 04:29.682
in our final answer.